    <Purpose>
        <div class="content">

            <p>
                RForge.Blazor.Events lets components talk to each other from anywhere in the tree, without a shared parent,
                without parameters passed down through every layer, and without a hand written service full of C# events.
                Derive from <code>RfEventComponent</code> and it takes care of subscribing, re-rendering on the right thread,
                error handling and disposal for you.
            </p>

            <p>
                It has two parts. <strong>Events</strong> (<code>IEvent</code>) are fire and forget messages that any component or
                service can publish and any component can handle. <strong>State</strong> (<code>IState&lt;T&gt;</code>) is a
                store-like value with a current value and change notification. Writing an equal value is a no-op, so record
                equality keeps components from re-rendering needlessly.
            </p>

            <h4>When to Use</h4>
            <p>
                When two components that are far apart in the tree need to share a value or react to the same thing: a cart badge
                that updates when a product card adds an item, a theme picker that restyles unrelated components, a toast host
                that any component can ask for a message.
            </p>

            <h4>Features</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Selectors:</strong> re-render only when the part of the state you use changes.</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Scopes:</strong> <code>EventScope</code> and <code>StateScope</code> isolate events and give subtrees
                    their own state. Routes include <code>Bubble</code> (the default), <code>Local</code>, <code>Broadcast</code>,
                    <code>Root</code>, <code>Global</code> and <code>Named</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Source generator:</strong> <code>[Subscribe]</code> and <code>[UseState]</code> need no reflection, so
                    they are trim and AOT safe. Analyzers (<code>RFEVT001</code> to <code>RFEVT014</code>) catch mistakes at build time.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Every render mode:</strong> static SSR, Interactive Server, WebAssembly and Auto, with or without
                    prerendering, and opt-in state persistence from prerender to interactive.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Plain components too:</strong> opt-in cascading values let third party components read state through
                    <code>[CascadingParameter]</code>.
                </li>
            </ul>

        </div>
    </Purpose>

    <Setup>
        <div class="content">

            <ol>
                <li>Install the package</li>
                <li>Register the event hub</li>
                <li>Make a component event-aware</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Install the Package</h2>

            <p>
                Install <code>RForge.Blazor.Events</code> for the events alone. The <code>RForge.Blazor</code> package installs the
                whole family (components, events and the auto API generator) instead.
            </p>

            <h6>Command Line</h6>
            <pre><code>dotnet add package RForge.Blazor.Events</code></pre>

            <h6>Package Manager Console</h6>
            <pre><code>Install-Package RForge.Blazor.Events</code></pre>

            <p>
                One package contains the runtime, the source generator and the analyzers. Add it to
                <strong>every project that contains event-aware components</strong>. In a Blazor Web App that is both the
                server project and the <code>.Client</code> project, because NuGet does not pass analyzers through project
                references.
            </p>

            <h2>Register the Event Hub</h2>

            <p>
                Call <code>AddRForgeEvents</code> in every <code>Program.cs</code> that hosts interactive components, including
                the <code>.Client</code> project for WebAssembly and Auto. Each runtime has its own container, so each needs its
                own hub.
            </p>

            <pre><code>builder.Services.AddRForgeEvents();</code></pre>

            <h2>Make a Component Event-aware</h2>

            <p>
                Start the <code>.razor</code> file with <code>@@inherits RfEventComponent</code> and put
                <code>[Subscribe]</code> and <code>[UseState]</code> members in the code-behind (<code>.razor.cs</code>)
                partial class, never in an <code>@@code</code> block. Source generators cannot see code that the Razor compiler
                produces, and <code>RFEVT001</code> reports it if you forget.
            </p>

            <h2>Requirements</h2>

            <table class="table is-striped">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Requirement</th>
                        <th>Details</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>.NET</td>
                        <td>.NET 8, 9 or 10. Events, state, scopes and the analyzers work with the .NET 8 SDK and newer.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>C# 13 for <code>[UseState]</code></td>
                        <td>
                            <code>[UseState]</code> is a partial property, which needs C# 13. net9 and net10 projects already
                            compile at C# 13 or later. A net8 project defaults to C# 12, so it needs the .NET 9 SDK or newer and
                            <code>&lt;LangVersion&gt;13&lt;/LangVersion&gt;</code> (or <code>latest</code>).
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Async lambdas at C# 12</td>
                        <td>
                            In a net8 project at C# 12, <code>builder.On&lt;MyEvent&gt;(async e =&gt; ...)</code> is ambiguous
                            between the <code>Task</code> and <code>ValueTask</code> overloads. Pass a method group, cast the
                            lambda to <code>Func&lt;MyEvent, Task&gt;</code>, or set <code>LangVersion</code> to 13 or later.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Server and WebAssembly islands</td>
                        <td>
                            Islands in different runtimes use different containers, so they never share a hub. See the
                            <a href="/events/example/render-modes">Render Modes example</a>.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2>Which Package Do I Install?</h2>

            <table class="table is-striped">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>You want</th>
                        <th>Install</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>The UI components only</td>
                        <td><code>RForge.Blazor.Components</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Typed events and state only</td>
                        <td><code>RForge.Blazor.Events</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>An API generated from a service interface</td>
                        <td><code>RForge.Blazor.AutoApi.Abstractions</code> and <code>RForge.Blazor.AutoApi.Generator</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Everything</td>
                        <td><code>RForge.Blazor</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

        </div>
    </Setup>

    <BasicUsage>
        <div class="content">

            <p>
                Define the events and state as records, publish and write from any component, and react anywhere else in the tree.
                The <a href="/events/tutorials">tutorials</a> walk through each part step by step.
            </p>

            <h2>Define Events and State</h2>

            <pre><code>public sealed record ItemAdded(int ProductId, int Quantity) : IEvent;

public sealed record CartState(int Count, decimal Total) : IState&lt;CartState&gt;
{
    public static CartState Initial =&gt; new(0, 0m);
}</code></pre>

            <h2>Publish and Write State</h2>

            <pre><code>// ProductCard.razor
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;button @@onclick="AddToCart"&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>

            <pre><code>// ProductCard.razor.cs
public partial class ProductCard
{
    [Parameter] public Product Product { get; set; } = default!;

    private async Task AddToCart()
    {
        State&lt;CartState&gt;().Update(s =&gt; s with { Count = s.Count + 1, Total = s.Total + Product.Price });
        await PublishAsync(new ItemAdded(Product.Id, 1));
    }
}</code></pre>

            <h2>React Anywhere Else</h2>

            <p>
                <code>CartBadge</code> needs no shared parent with <code>ProductCard</code> and no parameters. The selector
                makes it re-render only when the count changes.
            </p>

            <pre><code>// CartBadge.razor
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;span class="tag @@(_pulse ? "is-warning" : "")"&gt;@@Count&lt;/span&gt;</code></pre>

            <pre><code>// CartBadge.razor.cs
public partial class CartBadge
{
    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectCount))]
    private partial int Count { get; }

    private static int SelectCount(CartState s) =&gt; s.Count;

    [Subscribe]
    private void OnItemAdded(ItemAdded e) =&gt; _pulse = true;

    private bool _pulse;
}</code></pre>

            <h2>Scope a Subtree</h2>

            <p>
                Wrap a subtree in <code>EventScope</code> and <code>StateScope</code> so each instance gets its own event channel
                and its own copy of the state.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;EventScope Name="checkout"&gt;
    &lt;StateScope TState="CheckoutForm"&gt;
        &lt;AddressForm /&gt;
        &lt;PaymentForm /&gt;
    &lt;/StateScope&gt;
    &lt;OrderSummary /&gt;
&lt;/EventScope&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>Render Mode Support</h2>

            <table class="table is-striped">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Mode</th>
                        <th>Support</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Static SSR</td>
                        <td>Full within the request: one hub per request. Nothing updates after the HTML is sent.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Interactive Server</td>
                        <td>Full. One hub per circuit (per user).</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Interactive WebAssembly</td>
                        <td>Full. One hub per browser app instance.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Interactive Auto</td>
                        <td>Full, within whichever runtime the component is running in.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Prerendering</td>
                        <td>
                            Prerendered HTML reflects state loaded during the render. Opt-in <code>PersistState(...)</code> carries
                            root state into the interactive session.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Server and WebAssembly islands on one page</td>
                        <td>Not supported. Separate containers mean separate hubs.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2>Diagnostics</h2>

            <p>
                The analyzer that ships with the package reports these at build time. Errors stop the build; warnings and info
                point at likely bugs. <a href="/events/tutorials/12-troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> explains each fix.
            </p>

            <table class="table is-striped">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>ID</th>
                        <th>Severity</th>
                        <th>Meaning</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var diagnostic in Diagnostics)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>@diagnostic.Id</code></td>
                            <td>@diagnostic.Severity</td>
                            <td>@diagnostic.Meaning</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>

        </div>
    </BasicUsage>

</ComponentDetail>

@code {

    private static string EventsSource(string path) =>
        $"https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForge.Blazor.Events/{path}";

    private static readonly (string Id, string Severity, string Meaning)[] Diagnostics =
    [
        ("RFEVT001", "Error", "Attribute used in a .razor @code block. Move the member to the .razor.cs code-behind."),
        ("RFEVT002", "Error", "The type, or a type containing it, is not partial."),
        ("RFEVT003", "Error", "The type does not derive from RfEventComponent. Add @inherits RfEventComponent."),
        ("RFEVT004", "Error", "Invalid [Subscribe] signature."),
        ("RFEVT005", "Error", "The handler's first parameter is not an IEvent."),
        ("RFEVT006", "Error", "A [UseState] property is not a partial get-only declaration."),
        ("RFEVT007", "Error", "A [UseState] property type is not a state type. Use IState<T> or add Select."),
        ("RFEVT008", "Error", "Invalid selector."),
        ("RFEVT009", "Error", "Attributes and a RegisterSubscriptions override in the same component."),
        ("RFEVT010", "Warning", "Inconsistent Scope and ScopeName."),
        ("RFEVT011", "Warning", "Two handlers for the same event in one component."),
        ("RFEVT012", "Info", "The event type is not immutable. Use a record with init-only properties."),
        ("RFEVT013", "Warning", "The state type is not a record."),
        ("RFEVT014", "Warning", "The component implements IDisposable or IAsyncDisposable itself. Override DisposeAsyncCore instead."),
    ];

}
